+/**************************************************************************
+ *                                                                        *
+ *  Regina - A Normal Surface Theory Calculator                           *
+ *  Qt User Interface                                                     *
+ *                                                                        *
+ *  Weak fallbacks for OpenSSL 1.x protocol constructors.                 *
+ *                                                                        *
+ **************************************************************************/
+
+/**
+ * \file wasmopensslcompat.cpp
+ * \brief Weak fallback definitions for the OpenSSL 1.x protocol
+ * constructors that CPython's _ssl module still references.
+ *
+ * This file exists solely for the wasm32-emscripten build, where CPython
+ * is linked in statically (see PYTHON_STATIC_LINK).
+ *
+ * CPython's static module table names the PyInit_* entry point of every
+ * builtin extension module, so the linker pulls in _ssl.o whether or not
+ * anything will ever import the ssl module. _ssl.o in turn references
+ * TLSv1_method(), TLSv1_1_method() and TLSv1_2_method(): OpenSSL 1.x APIs
+ * that OpenSSL 3.x only exports when it is *not* configured with
+ * "no-deprecated". The OpenSSL package in this channel is configured that
+ * way, so these three symbols are missing from libssl.a/libcrypto.a and
+ * the static link fails.
+ *
+ * (This mismatch does not show up when CPython itself is built, because
+ * there the python executable links OpenSSL dynamically, where undefined
+ * symbols are resolved lazily rather than at link time.)
+ *
+ * The definitions below are declared weak, so if a future OpenSSL build
+ * in this channel *does* export the real functions, the real (strong)
+ * definitions win and these are ignored. Returning null is the correct
+ * failure mode in any case: an SSL_METHOD of null makes SSL_CTX_new()
+ * fail cleanly, and these three protocol versions are long deprecated --
+ * Python's own ssl module no longer offers PROTOCOL_TLSv1* by default.
+ * Regina's python console imports "regina" and nothing else, so in
+ * practice these are never called at all.
+ */
+
+#if defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)
+
+extern "C" {
+
+__attribute__((weak)) const void* TLSv1_method() { return nullptr; }
+__attribute__((weak)) const void* TLSv1_1_method() { return nullptr; }
+__attribute__((weak)) const void* TLSv1_2_method() { return nullptr; }
+
+} // extern "C"
+
+#endif
